{
  "dom_id": "step-e4132",
  "name": "Example: Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x) = 3(e^{5x}) + 2.$ Given that $f(x) \\to a$ as $x \\to -\\infty,$ then what is the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/b1e4758058c04e38d7077a22e5a6c0a1ff8ccab1596114bbb2517884261fe17c.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2 \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to -\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$\n\nFinally, we note that the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ is $y=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
